Chapter: GopDisk
  cites Works chapter GopAhci
  cites Works chapter GopNvme
  cites Works chapter GopUsbMsc

 Post-ExitBootServices disk reads. Once the boot stub calls
 ExitBootServices the firmware's Block I/O protocol is gone, so the
 payload must drive a controller itself. Two paths, tried in that order
 by `disk-read-sector`:

 AHCI (GopAhci, B3.1b) -- modern boards, q35, both machines in our
 matrix. Found by PCI class; the HBA fetches the sector by DMA.

 Legacy IDE PIO (this chapter, B3.1a) -- codex-vm's `-disk`, QEMU
 '-machine pc', old boards. Primary channel, ports 0x1F0-0x1F7.

 AHCI is tried first because a board with both usually presents the boot
 disk on the AHCI controller, and the legacy ports may answer for an
 empty compatibility channel.

 LBA28 READ SECTORS (command 0x20): select the drive with the top LBA
 nibble, program the count and 24-bit LBA, issue the command, then for
 each sector poll the status port until BSY clears and DRQ sets and
 read 256 16-bit words from the data port. Every poll loop is fuel-
 bounded so a missing or wedged disk fails to a status code instead of
 hanging; a program hosting this compiles with the pet flag anyway,
 like every boot-path loop.

 We say:

Section: Ports

  ide-data : Integer = 496
  ide-sect-count : Integer = 498
  ide-lba-lo : Integer = 499
  ide-lba-mid : Integer = 500
  ide-lba-hi : Integer = 501
  ide-drive : Integer = 502
  ide-command : Integer = 503
  ide-status : Integer = 503

  ide-st-bsy : Integer = 128
  ide-st-drq : Integer = 8
  ide-st-err : Integer = 1

  ide-cmd-read : Integer = 32
  ide-cmd-write : Integer = 48
  ide-cmd-flush : Integer = 231

  ide-poll-fuel : Integer = 100000

Section: Status Polling

 Wait for BSY to clear. Returns the last status byte read; the caller
 checks ERR. Fuel-bounded: exhaustion returns the busy status so the
 caller sees BSY still set and treats it as a failure.

  ide-wait-not-busy : Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  ide-wait-not-busy (fuel) = act
    s <- port-in-byte ide-status
    if bit-and s ide-st-bsy == 0 then s
    else if fuel <= 0 then s
    else ide-wait-not-busy (fuel - 1)
  end

  ide-wait-drq : Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  ide-wait-drq (fuel) = act
    s <- port-in-byte ide-status
    if bit-and s ide-st-bsy /= 0 then
      if fuel <= 0 then s else ide-wait-drq (fuel - 1)
    else if bit-and s ide-st-drq /= 0 then s
    else if bit-and s ide-st-err /= 0 then s
    else if fuel <= 0 then s
    else ide-wait-drq (fuel - 1)
  end

Section: Word Transfer

 Read 256 words (one 512-byte sector) from the data port into the
 buffer at addr. Bytes are stored little-endian, matching how the disk
 lays them out. Self-recursive over the word index -- constant stack.

  ide-read-words : Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  ide-read-words (addr) (i) = act
    if i >= 256 then 0
    else act
      w <- port-in-16 ide-data
      let lo = poke-byte addr (i * 2) (bit-and w 255)
      in let hi = poke-byte addr (i * 2 + 1) (bit-and (bit-shru w 8) 255)
      in ide-read-words addr (i + 1)
    end
  end

Section: Sector Read

 Read one LBA28 sector into a freshly-allocated 512-byte heap buffer.
 Returns the buffer address on success, or 0 on error (no disk, LBA out
 of range, or a poll timeout). The buffer is bump-allocated with
 __heap-advance; the caller owns it until its function returns.

  IdeRead = record {
    ir-ok : Boolean,
    ir-buf : Integer
  }

Section: Multi-Sector Read

 One command per sector costs a command per sector: a 2 MB seed is ~4200
 of them, and on IDE PIO each sector is 256 port reads. Both controllers
 take a sector count, so a bulk read asks once. AHCI carries the whole
 transfer in a single PRD (up to 4 MB); IDE is capped at 256 sectors per
 command by its 8-bit count register, so it walks the request in chunks.

  ide-max-chunk : Integer = 64

 Program the count and LBA once, then drain `count` sectors, waiting for
 DRQ before each. Returns 1 on success.

  ide-read-chunk : Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  ide-read-chunk (lba) (count) (dest) = act
    w0 <- ide-wait-not-busy ide-poll-fuel
    dh <- port-out-byte ide-drive (bit-or 224 (bit-and (bit-shru lba 24) 15))
    sc <- port-out-byte ide-sect-count count
    l0 <- port-out-byte ide-lba-lo (bit-and lba 255)
    l1 <- port-out-byte ide-lba-mid (bit-and (bit-shru lba 8) 255)
    l2 <- port-out-byte ide-lba-hi (bit-and (bit-shru lba 16) 255)
    cm <- port-out-byte ide-command ide-cmd-read
    ide-drain-sectors dest 0 count
  end

  ide-drain-sectors : Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  ide-drain-sectors (dest) (i) (count) = act
    if i >= count then 1
    else act
      s <- ide-wait-drq ide-poll-fuel
      if bit-and s ide-st-drq == 0 then 0
      else act
        r <- ide-read-words (dest + i * 512) 0
        ide-drain-sectors dest (i + 1) count
      end
    end
  end

 A machine with no legacy IDE controller floats the bus: every status
 read answers 0xFF, which fakes BSY, DRQ, and ERR all at once. The
 fuel-bounded waits then time out holding 0xFF, the DRQ test passes,
 and the drain loop "reads" sectors of all-ones and reports success --
 a phantom disk full of garbage that outranks the real one in the
 dispatch. All-ones is the same floating-bus signature the i8042 and
 LAPIC probes retire on; one status read up front retires the whole
 controller.

  ide-absent : [Device.Port] Boolean
  ide-absent = act
    s <- port-in-byte ide-status
    s == 255
  end

  ide-read-into : Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  ide-read-into (lba) (count) (dest) = act
    absent <- ide-absent
    if absent then 0
    else ide-read-run lba count dest
  end

  ide-read-run : Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  ide-read-run (lba) (count) (dest) = act
    if count <= 0 then 1
    else act
      let n = if count > ide-max-chunk then ide-max-chunk else count
      in act
        ok <- ide-read-chunk lba n dest
        if ok == 0 then 0
        else ide-read-run (lba + n) (count - n) (dest + n * 512)
      end
    end
  end

Section: Medium Selection

 Dispatch order answers "which controller responds", not "which
 medium is mine" -- and on a real machine the internal SATA disk
 responds on AHCI before the boot stick responds on USB, so every
 read above this layer would come from the WRONG disk. The selection
 cells fix the medium by content: GopMedium probes each candidate
 (does its ESP carry our own CODEX.CDX?) by steering these cells and
 locks the winner. While the magic is present, every read and write
 in this chapter goes straight to the selected medium; without it,
 the dispatch order below stands, which is also how the probe itself
 reads. The magic is written LAST, so firmware residue in the cells
 cannot impersonate a selection.

  med-cells : Integer = 36608
  med-cells-magic : Integer = #3144454D

  med-kind-ahci : Integer = 1
  med-kind-ide : Integer = 2
  med-kind-usb : Integer = 3
  med-kind-nvme : Integer = 4

  med-selected : Integer
  med-selected =
    if peek-32 med-cells 0 /= med-cells-magic then 0
    else peek-32 med-cells 4

  med-sel-index : Integer
  med-sel-index = peek-32 med-cells 8

  med-set : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  med-set (kind) (index) =
    let k = poke-32 med-cells 4 kind
    in let i = poke-32 med-cells 8 index
    in poke-32 med-cells 0 med-cells-magic

  med-clear : Integer -> Integer
  med-clear (dummy) = poke-32 med-cells 0 0

 Bulk read through the selected medium when one is locked, else
 through whichever controller answers. AHCI is tried first and does
 the whole thing in one DMA; the IDE fallback chunks it; USB mass
 storage is last because its bring-up is the heaviest and boards
 present fixed disks on the other two. On a machine that booted from
 a USB stick with no other disk, USB is the only path that answers.

  disk-read-selected : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Boolean
  disk-read-selected (sel) (lba) (count) (dest) = act
    if sel == med-kind-ahci then act
      hba <- ahci-find-nth med-sel-index
      if hba.hba-ok then ahci-read-into-on hba lba count dest /= 0
      else False
    end
    else if sel == med-kind-nvme then act
      n <- nvme-read-into lba count dest
      n /= 0
    end
    else if sel == med-kind-ide then act
      i <- ide-read-into lba count dest
      i /= 0
    end
    else act
      u <- usb-read-into lba count dest
      u /= 0
    end
  end

  disk-read-into : Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Boolean
  disk-read-into (lba) (count) (dest) = act
    let sel = med-selected
    in if sel /= 0 then disk-read-selected sel lba count dest
    else act
      a <- ahci-read-into lba count dest
      if a /= 0 then True
      else act
        n <- nvme-read-into lba count dest
        if n /= 0 then True
        else act
          i <- ide-read-into lba count dest
          if i /= 0 then True
          else act
            u <- usb-read-into lba count dest
            u /= 0
          end
        end
      end
    end
  end

Section: Multi-Sector Write

 Writing mirrors reading: program the LBA and count once, then push each
 sector's 256 words to the data port, waiting for DRQ before each. After
 the last sector a FLUSH CACHE forces the drive to commit -- without it a
 write can sit in a volatile cache and a power loss loses the identity we
 just saved.

  ide-write-words : Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  ide-write-words (addr) (i) = act
    if i >= 256 then 0
    else act
      let lo = peek-byte addr (i * 2)
      in let hi = peek-byte addr (i * 2 + 1)
      in act
        w <- port-out-16 ide-data (bit-or lo (bit-shl hi 8))
        ide-write-words addr (i + 1)
      end
    end
  end

  ide-fill-sectors : Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  ide-fill-sectors (src) (i) (count) = act
    if i >= count then 1
    else act
      s <- ide-wait-drq ide-poll-fuel
      if bit-and s ide-st-drq == 0 then 0
      else act
        w <- ide-write-words (src + i * 512) 0
        ide-fill-sectors src (i + 1) count
      end
    end
  end

  ide-write-chunk : Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  ide-write-chunk (lba) (count) (src) = act
    w0 <- ide-wait-not-busy ide-poll-fuel
    dh <- port-out-byte ide-drive (bit-or 224 (bit-and (bit-shru lba 24) 15))
    sc <- port-out-byte ide-sect-count count
    l0 <- port-out-byte ide-lba-lo (bit-and lba 255)
    l1 <- port-out-byte ide-lba-mid (bit-and (bit-shru lba 8) 255)
    l2 <- port-out-byte ide-lba-hi (bit-and (bit-shru lba 16) 255)
    cm <- port-out-byte ide-command ide-cmd-write
    ide-fill-sectors src 0 count
  end

  ide-flush : [Device.Port] Integer
  ide-flush = act
    c <- port-out-byte ide-command ide-cmd-flush
    ide-wait-not-busy ide-poll-fuel
  end

  ide-write-into : Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  ide-write-into (lba) (count) (src) = act
    absent <- ide-absent
    if absent then 0
    else ide-write-run lba count src
  end

  ide-write-run : Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  ide-write-run (lba) (count) (src) = act
    if count <= 0 then 1
    else act
      let n = if count > ide-max-chunk then ide-max-chunk else count
      in act
        ok <- ide-write-chunk lba n src
        if ok == 0 then 0
        else ide-write-run (lba + n) (count - n) (src + n * 512)
      end
    end
  end

 Bulk write through the selected medium when one is locked, else
 through whichever controller answers. AHCI DMAs the whole run; IDE
 chunks it and issues one flush at the end; USB rides last, same
 order as reads -- a write must land on the same medium a read would
 have come from.

  disk-write-selected : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Boolean
  disk-write-selected (sel) (lba) (count) (src) = act
    if sel == med-kind-ahci then act
      hba <- ahci-find-nth med-sel-index
      if hba.hba-ok then ahci-issue-on hba lba count src ahci-cmd-write True /= 0
      else False
    end
    else if sel == med-kind-nvme then act
      n <- nvme-write-into lba count src
      n /= 0
    end
    else if sel == med-kind-ide then act
      i <- ide-write-into lba count src
      if i /= 0 then act
        fl <- ide-flush
        True
      end
      else False
    end
    else act
      u <- usb-write-into lba count src
      u /= 0
    end
  end

  disk-write-into : Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] Boolean
  disk-write-into (lba) (count) (src) = act
    let sel = med-selected
    in if sel /= 0 then disk-write-selected sel lba count src
    else act
      a <- ahci-write-into lba count src
      if a /= 0 then True
      else act
        n <- nvme-write-into lba count src
        if n /= 0 then True
        else act
          i <- ide-write-into lba count src
          if i /= 0 then act
            fl <- ide-flush
            True
          end
          else act
            u <- usb-write-into lba count src
            u /= 0
          end
        end
      end
    end
  end

Section: Dispatch

 Try the selected medium, else AHCI, then legacy IDE, then USB mass
 storage. `dr-via` names the controller that answered so the caller
 can say which path reached the disk -- on a boot screen that
 distinction is the difference between "we read the stick" and "we
 read something".

  DiskRead = record {
    dr-ok : Boolean,
    dr-buf : Integer,
    dr-via : Text
  }

  disk-read-sector-selected : Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port] DiskRead
  disk-read-sector-selected (sel) (lba) = act
    let buf = alloc-zeroed 512 64
    in act
      ok <- disk-read-selected sel lba 1 buf
      if ok == False then DiskRead { dr-ok = False, dr-buf = 0, dr-via = "none" }
      else if sel == med-kind-ahci then DiskRead { dr-ok = True, dr-buf = buf, dr-via = "AHCI" }
      else if sel == med-kind-nvme then DiskRead { dr-ok = True, dr-buf = buf, dr-via = "NVMe" }
      else if sel == med-kind-ide then DiskRead { dr-ok = True, dr-buf = buf, dr-via = "IDE PIO" }
      else DiskRead { dr-ok = True, dr-buf = buf, dr-via = "USB" }
    end
  end

  disk-read-sector : Integer -> [Device.Port] DiskRead
  disk-read-sector (lba) = act
    let sel = med-selected
    in if sel /= 0 then disk-read-sector-selected sel lba
    else disk-read-sector-order lba
  end

  disk-read-sector-order : Integer -> [Device.Port] DiskRead
  disk-read-sector-order (lba) = act
    a <- ahci-read-sector lba
    if a.ar-ok then DiskRead { dr-ok = True, dr-buf = a.ar-buf, dr-via = "AHCI" }
    else act
      nbuf <- nvme-read-sector lba
      if nbuf /= 0 then DiskRead { dr-ok = True, dr-buf = nbuf, dr-via = "NVMe" }
      else act
        i <- ide-read-sector lba
        if i.ir-ok then DiskRead { dr-ok = True, dr-buf = i.ir-buf, dr-via = "IDE PIO" }
        else act
          let buf = alloc-zeroed 512 64
          in act
            u <- usb-read-into lba 1 buf
            if u /= 0 then DiskRead { dr-ok = True, dr-buf = buf, dr-via = "USB" }
            else DiskRead { dr-ok = False, dr-buf = 0, dr-via = "none" }
          end
        end
      end
    end
  end

Section: IDE Sector Read

  ide-read-sector : Integer -> [Device.Port] IdeRead
  ide-read-sector (lba) = act
    absent <- ide-absent
    if absent then IdeRead { ir-ok = False, ir-buf = 0 }
    else ide-read-sector-run lba
  end

  ide-read-sector-run : Integer -> [Device.Port] IdeRead
  ide-read-sector-run (lba) = act
    w0 <- ide-wait-not-busy ide-poll-fuel
    dh <- port-out-byte ide-drive (bit-or 224 (bit-and (bit-shru lba 24) 15))
    sc <- port-out-byte ide-sect-count 1
    l0 <- port-out-byte ide-lba-lo (bit-and lba 255)
    l1 <- port-out-byte ide-lba-mid (bit-and (bit-shru lba 8) 255)
    l2 <- port-out-byte ide-lba-hi (bit-and (bit-shru lba 16) 255)
    cm <- port-out-byte ide-command ide-cmd-read
    s <- ide-wait-drq ide-poll-fuel
    if bit-and s ide-st-drq == 0 then IdeRead { ir-ok = False, ir-buf = 0 }
    else act
      let buf = __heap-save
      in let adv = __heap-advance 512
      in act
        r <- ide-read-words buf 0
        IdeRead { ir-ok = True, ir-buf = buf }
      end
    end
  end

Section: Identify

 IDENTIFY DEVICE (0xEC) answers one 512-byte sector describing the
 drive: the model string sits at words 27-46 and the sector counts at
 words 60-61 (LBA28) and 100-103 (LBA48). No LBA registers to program;
 select the drive, issue, drain one sector. Behind the same floating-bus
 gate as every other IDE entry, and a DRQ that never arrives (no device
 on a present controller) returns zero instead of a garbage page.

  ide-cmd-identify : Integer = 236

  ide-identify : [Device.Port] Integer
  ide-identify = act
    absent <- ide-absent
    if absent then 0
    else act
      w0 <- ide-wait-not-busy ide-poll-fuel
      dh <- port-out-byte ide-drive 224
      cm <- port-out-byte ide-command ide-cmd-identify
      s <- ide-wait-drq ide-poll-fuel
      if bit-and s ide-st-drq == 0 then 0
      else act
        let buf = alloc-zeroed 512 64
        in act
          r <- ide-read-words buf 0
          buf
        end
      end
    end
  end
